GoSD examples/chime: default snd_bcm2835's HDMI audio device to enabled.

The driver only asks the VideoCore firmware which displays are live - and so
only creates the "bcm2835 HDMI 1"/"HDMI 2" ALSA cards - when its enable_hdmi
module parameter is set, and that parameter defaults to off:

    if (enable_hdmi && !of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node, "brcm,disable-hdmi"))
            set_hdmi_enables(dev);

GoSD kernels are monolithic, so the only way to set a module parameter is the
kernel command line, and nothing in a `gosd build-kernel` recipe can put it
there on every board:

  - `dtparam=audio=on` would do it on pi-zero-2w and pi-3b, whose downstream
    DTBs carry an __overrides__ entry that rewrites chosen's bootargs - but an
    example cannot add lines to the board's config.txt (see bean gosd-mf3a),
    and pi-zero-w's mainline-style DTB has no __overrides__ node at all.
  - CONFIG_CMDLINE + CONFIG_CMDLINE_EXTEND works on arch/arm (pi-zero-w) and
    is simply absent on arch/arm64: that Kconfig offers only
    CMDLINE_FROM_BOOTLOADER and CMDLINE_FORCE, and forcing would throw away
    the console=/init=/gosd.board= arguments gosd-init needs.

Changing the default is therefore the one lever that behaves identically on
all three Pi boards. It only affects which cards are *offered*:
set_hdmi_enables() still asks the firmware, so a board with no display
attached still gets no HDMI card. An app that wants the old behaviour can pass
snd_bcm2835.enable_hdmi=0.

--- a/drivers/staging/vc04_services/bcm2835-audio/bcm2835.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/vc04_services/bcm2835-audio/bcm2835.c
@@ -11,7 +11,12 @@
 #include "bcm2835.h"
 #include <soc/bcm2835/raspberrypi-firmware.h>
 
-static bool enable_hdmi, enable_hdmi0, enable_hdmi1;
+/*
+ * GoSD examples/chime: HDMI audio on by default, because a monolithic kernel
+ * has no way to pass a module parameter that works on every Pi board.
+ */
+static bool enable_hdmi = true;
+static bool enable_hdmi0, enable_hdmi1;
 static bool enable_headphones = true;
 static int num_channels = MAX_SUBSTREAMS;
 
